README for corelib::progs/eval/bivariateRange

Dec 2025:
	This folder contains all the code and data reported in our paper,
		"Range Functions of Any Convergence Order and
					their Amortized Complexity Analysis"
			by
		Kai Hormann, Chee Yap and Ya Shi Zhang

	 (paper was submitted to GMP 2026)

BINGWEI: please update the following:
	
	*A subfolder "data" contains of a suite of real polynomials
			(chebyshev, hermite, mignotte, wilkinson, sparse)
		of various degrees.  These are square-free polynomials.
	* We implemented the EVAL algorithm for isolating the
		real roots of a polynomial f(x) in a given interval [a,b].
	* There are 12 versions of EVAL, depending of the type of range
			function used to evaluate f(x) and f'(x):

		 evalH4			-- Hermite order 4
		 evalH4cheap	-- cheap version of Hermite order 4
		 evalH4ncheap	-- non-maximal level of cheap Hermite order 4
		 evalL3			-- Lagrange order 3
		 evalL3cheap 	-- cheap version of Lagrange order 3
		 evalL3ncheap 	-- non-maximal level of cheap Lagrange order 3
		 evalL4			-- Lagrange order 4
		 evalL4cheap	-- non-maximal level of cheap Lagrange order 4
		 EvalT2			-- Taylor order 2
		 evalT2min 		-- minimal level Taylor order 2
		 evalT3			-- Taylor order 3
		 evalT4		 	-- Taylor order 4
	* We have 5 experiments, producing 3 tables and 2 figures
		 Table1: shows the sizes of the subdivision trees for various
		 			EVAL methods
		 Table2: shows the average running time when the 
				arithmetic uses 1024-bit machine floating point 
		 Table3: shows the average running time when the
				arithmetic uses multi-precision rational numbers
		 Figure1: shows the speedup ratio of cheap Hermite order 4
					against cheap Lagrange order 3 for different families
					of polynomial (of various degrees on x-axis)
				[Figure1b is a smoothed version of Figure1a]
		 Figure2: shows the speedup ratio of non-maximal level cheap
		 		Lagrange order 3 against the maximal level.
				The level varies along the x-axis. 
				Figure2a and 2b uses polynomials of degrees of 125 and
				250 (respectively).
					The same experiment is repeated with
		 		cheap Lagrange order 3 method in Figure2c and 2d. 
	* We have a Makefile with the targets:
			table1, table2, table3, figure1 and figure2
		to automatically produce these tables and figures.
